## Temporary files

Temporary files go in `tmp/` only. The scripts set `TMPDIR` and `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES`
to it for themselves.<!-- if CLAUDE_CODE --> `.claude/settings.local.json` sets them for
every command Claude Code runs.<!-- end CLAUDE_CODE --> For other commands, set them
yourself, from the repository's root:

```sh
export TMPDIR="$PWD/tmp" GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES="$PWD/tmp"
```

- Most tools put their temporary files in `TMPDIR`. macOS `mktemp -d` ignores it, so give
  it a template: `mktemp -d "$PWD/tmp/name.XXXXXX"`.
- `GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES` stops git, in a folder under `tmp/`, from walking up into this
  repository. Tests that create git repositories there need it.
- Node keeps a compile cache in `TMPDIR`, at `tmp/node-compile-cache`.

## Commits, versions and releases

Commit messages follow Conventional Commits (https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/):
`feat:` for a feature, `fix:` for a fix, and a `!` or a `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer for a
breaking change. Versions follow Semantic Versioning. `CHANGELOG.md` has an Unreleased
section, which each iteration fills in, and the release script turns into the new
version's section.

Make a release only when I ask for one.

```sh
scripts/release.sh --dry-run X.Y.Z   # show what releasing would do
scripts/release.sh X.Y.Z             # release to GitHub: version, changelog, tag and GitHub release
<!-- if PUBLISH -->
scripts/publish.sh --dry-run         # show what publishing would do
scripts/publish.sh                   # publish the release to {{PUBLISH_TARGETS}}
<!-- end PUBLISH -->
scripts/rollback.sh X.Y.Z            # undo a GitHub release that nothing has published yet
```
<!-- if PUBLISH -->

Publishing to a registry can't be undone.
<!-- end PUBLISH -->

