## 2.0 Overall work process

Our work is organized in iterations, following `docs/planning/ROADMAP.md`. An iteration
holds single backlog items, one or more groups of items, or both. After an iteration (section 2.1) is completed,
we run these stages:

1. Update the documentation and the Unreleased section of `CHANGELOG.md`.
2. Make the release.
3. Tidy up the backlog and the roadmap.
4. Prepare for the next iteration. At this point you pause, and wait for my signal to
   continue.

`ROADMAP.md` is a document of ideas. It never holds sensitive information, which belongs in a
backlog item. It follows `docs/templates/roadmap.md`.

## 2.1 Iterations

Before an iteration starts, we agree which single items and groups from the backlog to
include. Iterations
are numbered `iteration-NNN`, where NNN is an incremental number, and each one has a
document at `docs/iterations/NNN/iteration-NNN.md`. Keep the document purposely concise,
with a list of what it includes.

Start each one from `docs/templates/iteration.md`.

An iteration is DONE when all its items are built, tested, reviewed, documented and
verified.

## 2.2 Project backlog

The backlog is `docs/planning/backlog/BACKLOG.md`, our index and database of items. Each
item is one unit of work, with an optional list of subtasks, and is marked OPEN,
IN_PROGRESS, DONE, BLOCKED or PARKED. The roadmap's items use the same statuses.

Each item is a Markdown document in `docs/planning/backlog/items/`, named
`{NNNN}-{title}.md`, where NNNN is an incremental identifier that never changes.
`BACKLOG.md` is only the index and overview. When we tidy up and groom the backlog, or
reprioritize items, you update the Priority field in each item's metadata table (section
2.2.1).

An item can belong to one group, a named batch of work, through the Group field in its
metadata. Group names are lower-case words joined by hyphens, such as `search-rewrite`. An
item outside any group has `-` there, and is a single item. An iteration can include single
items, one or more groups, or both. Including a group includes all its items that aren't
DONE, BLOCKED or PARKED.

An item marked DONE is never reopened. When bugs, updates or refactoring touch code that
an item delivered, we open new items in the backlog, and add references to the relevant
older items in their metadata tables.

### 2.2.1 Backlog item template

Start each item from `docs/templates/backlog-item.md`.

## 2.3 Our work process

### 2.3.1 When ROADMAP.md is empty or all its items are DONE

1. We brainstorm ideas.
2. We agree which of the ideas go into `ROADMAP.md`, as the project's strategy, vision
   and goals.

### 2.3.2 When BACKLOG.md is empty or all its items are DONE

1. We agree which of the open ideas in the roadmap to build.
2. We create backlog items and index them in `BACKLOG.md`. Each item is created from the
   template in section 2.2.1, with its first tasks.
3. Later, when an iteration starts, each item's task list is revisited, verified and
   updated.

### 2.3.3 When BACKLOG.md has open items

1. Suggest the single items and groups for the next iteration, leaving out items that are
   DONE, BLOCKED or PARKED. This starts a new iteration.
2. Design a solution. Analyse the source code, and identify the significant
   requirements, and the possible solutions and their trade-offs when there is more than
   one. Consider an external library or framework only when the language's standard
   library can't provide what the implementation needs.
3. Save the design at `docs/architecture/design/iteration-NNN/{title}.md`, from the
   template in section 2.4.1.
4. A design document can reference other design documents. Keep it concise and brief,
   without long paragraphs or unnecessary detail. It may contain Mermaid diagrams,
   including Mermaid C4 models, but only where they are needed.
5. Check the design document against the writing rules in section 2.5, then ask
   `application-architect` for a design review.
6. Wait for the review, verify what it found, and make the necessary updates to the
   design document.
7. Add your questions to me about the design, if you have any, to the design document.
8. Present the design and your questions to me, and wait for my input. From my feedback,
   update the design's solution, decisions and log, and record each decision that will
   outlive the iteration as an ADR (section 2.4.2). Make sure the task lists match the
   updated design, the review and my input. Then wait for my signal to continue.
9. Implement, test and build all the tasks in the iteration's items. Work directly on the
   main branch (`{{MAIN_BRANCH}}`) unless we agree otherwise.
10. When all the items of the iteration are done, ask `application-architect` and
    `security-architect` for a code review.<!-- if FRONTEND --> Only if the frontend
    changed, also ask `lead-frontend-developer` to review the frontend code (the changes
    in `{{FRONTEND_DIR}}/`), and `product-ux-designer` to review the design.<!-- end FRONTEND -->
11. Wait for all the reviews to finish, then read all the feedback. Fix everything you can
    right away, and make new backlog items for issues that need more analysis or a new
    design review.
12. When all the issues from the reviews are fixed, show me the items you created, if
    any, and wait for my input. We decide on and prioritize the new items, and consider
    whether to fix them now or plan them for another iteration. Fixes made at this point
    are code reviewed too (steps 9 to 12).
13. Finally, when everything is implemented, we tidy up, remove temporary files, and make
    a new release.

### 2.3.4 Backlog grooming

1. After each iteration, we update the roadmap and the backlog. We update the statuses and
   the groups, and reprioritize where what we have learned calls for it.

## 2.4 Architecture and security documents

### 2.4.1 Design documents

Save each design document at `docs/architecture/design/iteration-NNN/{title}.md`, starting
from `docs/templates/design-document.md`.

### 2.4.2 Architecture decision records

Save each decision at `docs/architecture/decisions/NNNN-{title}.md`, where NNNN counts up
and never changes. Once a decision is accepted, its text is never edited. A new decision
supersedes it, and only its status changes.

Start each one from `docs/templates/decision-record.md`.

### 2.4.3 Security documents

`security-architect` keeps `docs/security/threat-model.md` and `docs/security/standards.md`
current. The setup writes both with the sections to fill in.<!-- if PUBLIC --> They are public, so
they describe threats and mitigations, never an unfixed vulnerability.<!-- end PUBLIC -->

## 2.5 Writing

These rules apply to documents, logs, commit messages and pull requests:

- Say the thing directly. Don't set it up with a contrast ("not X but Y"), an announcement
  that a point is coming, or a closing line that repeats it.
- Make every sentence add something the reader doesn't have yet. Keep every fact, and never
  invent one.
- Use plain words, without inflated claims, filler or sales language.
- Use commas, colons and periods, not em or en dashes.
- Use sentence-case headings, and no bold labels at the start of list items.
